- **Buyer/trigger:** expecting parents or parents of a baby under 6 months drawn to Montessori ("I wish I'd known about Montessori earlier"), usually via the author's bestselling book *The Montessori Baby*.
- **Mechanism (≤8 words):** Prepared home environment, follow the baby.
- **Promise:** a gentle way to parent; set up the home "with less items than you think"; understand movement and language development; guide activities.
- **Proof:** author brand (her books); 20% of profits donated; no outcome claims.
- **Price:** Montessori for Babies (0–1 year) US$29, 2-hour recorded workshop + Q&A, lifetime access; school licences US$145 (staff) and US$500 (parents). Catalogue "from US$10 to US$399": Introduction US$10; age workshops US$29 each or bundle "save 20%"; "52 weeks of Montessori" US$49 (one digital card by email each week for a year); masterclasses US$29 each.
- **Format:** recorded live webinars, emailed card deck, courses on a Teachable-style platform.
- **Personalization:** by age band only (babies / toddlers / preschoolers / 6–12).
- **Guarantee:** none stated on captured pages.
- **Angles and hooks:** "gentle", "independent, caring child", fewer toys, home setup; weekly drip ("one card in your inbox every week").
- **Objections answered:** missed the live session (recording); accessibility (captions); come back as the child grows.

Verbatim:

> This 2-hour online workshop is especially for people who are expecting a baby or have a child under 6 months old.

> - how to set up your home for your baby with less items than you think

> Buy now and get immediate access. And you have UNLIMITED access to this workshop.

> 52 weeks of Montessori (US$49) – one card in your inbox every week with a new theme to practise, helping you deeply integrate Montessori in your home

> Online courses from US$10 to US$399

## Comparison with Mamivio — interpretation

The best-known Montessori-at-home author prices the baby year at US$29 for a single 2-hour workshop and sells a 52-week email drip at US$49 — a weekly "one thing to practise" format that a month-by-month guide resembles. Montessori buyers want fewer toys and a prepared space more than an activity list; a Mamivio guide that prescribes products or busy activities would clash with them.

## Gaps

Course content not seen; sales volume unknown; book prices not captured.

