 Mastering Naps and Schedules – 
an e-Book from The Baby Sleep Site™ 

 For many babies and toddlers, sleep is a struggle and a chore. This, of course, naturally means that it’s a struggle and a chore for you, too! You might feel like all you do is spend your entire day trying to get your baby or toddler to nap. It doesn’t matter whether you’re struggling with baby schedules for bedtime at night or toddler schedules for naptime during the day – all you know is your child needs sleep, you want sleep, and no one ever seems to be getting enough. 

 The good news is, one of the primary causes of poor sleeping habits in toddlers and babies is not an inherently “bad” baby or the “wrong” parenting style. In most cases, WHEN you are trying a nap can make the biggest difference in how long your baby naps and poor night sleep can improve, too. Baby naps – those wonderful, luxurious snatches of sleep that many parents look forward to for hours – are often the perfect solution for everyone in your house to be able to get the rest they need.

 “”I purchased your book and it helped a great deal. Our daughter is sleeping well through the night – that actually wasn’t our problem. Nap time was the issue and I was beside myself with anguish over it. Your book was great and your email responses were very helpful. We even took a trip to California and back and the napping routine remained fairly consistent. She falls asleep on her own now without fussing and is sleeping 3-4 hours during the day and 11-12 hours at night. Thank you so much!”

-Heather and Jay, Washington, D.C.

 The Nap / Night Connection 

 For most parents, eliminating or reducing baby and toddler naps seems like a good idea. After all, it logically follows that the less sleep they get during the day, the more they’ll need at night…and the more they need at night, the better your chances of a deep, uninterrupted night’s rest. Right?

 Wrong. Unfortunately, this is one of the few cases in parenthood when your instincts aren’t reliable. According to most parenting experts, babies as young as four months old and toddlers well into their toddling years all need a good, solid foundation of sleep during the day in order to get the sleep they need at night.

 The truth is that the better rested babies and toddlers are during the day, the better they’ll fall asleep – and stay asleep . Overtired children are much more prone to struggle with their bed
